  {
    "number": 29,
    "title": "Job, Career, or Empire",
    "part": "Part Five · Staying Human",
    "blocks": [
      {
        "type": "p",
        "text": "2 9 / S TAY I N G H U M A N EMPIRE"
      },
      {
        "type": "p",
        "text": "We are busy every day. Busy pursuing what, exactly? Rarely do we stop long enough to ask the question honestly: what are we actually pursuing, and does what we do most days actually move us toward it? For most senior executives, the honest answer falls into one of three pursuits, whether we have ever said it out loud or not."
      },
      {
        "type": "subhead",
        "text": "Pursuing a Job"
      },
      {
        "type": "p",
        "text": "In the job we currently hold, we spend our energy consolidating our position inside the company, because we prefer our comfort zone. Another signal is that we cannot imagine what it would be like to leave, and we are not confident about getting the same or higher pay if we did. So we settle in, and decide to climb only this one company's ladder."
      },
      {
        "type": "subhead",
        "text": "Pursuing a Career"
      },
      {
        "type": "p",
        "text": "A career looks like more. It comes with promotions, broader scope, a title that means something at the next company too. Most senior corporate professionals fall into this category, and it is also the least stable of the three pursuits."
      },
      {
        "type": "p",
        "text": "However, it's impossible to establish substantial outcomes without staying long enough in the same company, and that means: the jumping has to stop at a certain point. And a high salary makes it easy for us to land in the first round when layoffs"
      },
      {
        "type": "p",
        "text": "come. Pursuing a career looks glamorous, but it carries its own risk: finding ourselves unemployed in midlife."
      },
      {
        "type": "subhead",
        "text": "Pursuing our Own Empire"
      },
      {
        "type": "p",
        "text": "Unlike pursuing a job, being confined to the company system scares us, and we have zero interest in office politics. Our focus is doing what feels meaningful, and building something of our own. It was never just about the title of being the boss: what we actually want is to call the shots, so we can make happen whatever we set out to do. We prefer the idea that the sky is no limit, and our fortune shouldn't be, either. Regardless, while we are not ready yet and still on the job, we make good use of the time: gathering resources and connections, and planning our exit before we get too comfortable to leave."
      },
      {
        "type": "p",
        "text": "None of the three pursuits is wrong. What matters is naming the one we truly desire, and then checking honestly whether what we do most days is actually moving us toward it, or simply keeps us busy. Busy is not the same as arriving."
      },
      {
        "type": "p",
        "text": "Nothing we pursue becomes real until we are honest enough to name it."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "number": 30,
    "title": "A Fulfilling Life",
    "part": "Part Five · Staying Human",
    "blocks": [
      {
        "type": "p",
        "text": "3 0 / S TAY I N G H U M A N FULFILLING"
      },
      {
        "type": "p",
        "text": "Years ago, I sat across from a senior professor, listening to him explain, without a trace of shyness, that he had decided early what he wanted for his life: fame and power. So he was willing to take a university salary, basically a fraction of what the private sector could have offered, in exchange for a very high seat in that university. He told me to figure out the same thing for myself, sooner rather than later. What struck me was not only the choice he'd made, but that he'd said it out loud. Almost nobody admits, especially to a stranger, that what they want is fame and power. I respected him for the clarity he'd reached."
      },
      {
        "type": "subhead",
        "text": "Give and Take"
      },
      {
        "type": "p",
        "text": "We talked about pursuit in the previous chapter: what we do for a living can be the pursuit of a stable job, a career of title and satisfying compensation, or an empire of our own. Now we broaden the dimension to our lives as a whole. What is the pursuit of our life? Money, fame, power, freedom, happy relationships, or family? We may want it all, or at least several of them. There is always give and take in the journey. A fulfilling life starts with getting honest about which of these actually make us feel satisfied, not addicted."
      },
      {
        "type": "subhead",
        "text": "Align What we Do with our Pursuit of Life"
      },
      {
        "type": "p",
        "text": "Money is often just the ticket that propels the rest of the list forward: it funds the business, pays for making change in the world, and allows us to take care of the people we love and watch them smile. For most of us, work is only ever a means to a"
      },
      {
        "type": "p",
        "text": "set of life goals, which is why, every time we catch ourselves in a work problem, stepping back to that bigger picture helps us ask whether this particular fight deserves our time, our thought, our emotion, and our health."
      },
      {
        "type": "subhead",
        "text": "The Foundation of All: Health"
      },
      {
        "type": "p",
        "text": "Every item on the goal list requires one thing to be worth it: a healthy body capable of enjoying it. Money means nothing to someone too sick to spend it. Freedom means nothing to someone who can only lie in bed. Whatever we decide to prioritize, health cannot be the item we give up to get there. Mental health always shows up in physical health eventually. And that's why this book spent so many pages working on strengthening readers' mental health at work."
      },
      {
        "type": "p",
        "text": "Being an outsmarter means knowing how to play the game, how to play the man, and, most importantly, what we are playing for."
      }
    ]
  }
